WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.940
<v Maya>Nobody remembers slide forty-seven.</v>

00:00:02.380 --> 00:00:04.520
<v Marcus>But everybody remembers a story.</v>

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.380
<v Maya>I'm Maya, he's Marcus, and this is Chief of Staff Confidential.</v>

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.420
<v Maya>Today: storytelling.</v>

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.220
<v Maya>Which, I'll be honest, sounds soft for a show about getting things done.</v>

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:18.220
<v Marcus>It's the opposite of soft.</v>

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:20.880
<v Marcus>It's the hardest-working skill you have.</v>

00:00:21.680 --> 00:00:22.940
<v Marcus>Data informs people.</v>

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:25.180
<v Marcus>Stories persuade them.</v>

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:29.020
<v Marcus>You can put perfect numbers in front of a room and get nods.</v>

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.500
<v Marcus>Tell the story behind the numbers and you get action.</v>

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:39.700
<v Marcus>The chief of staff sits exactly where strategy meets humans, and stories are how you move humans.</v>

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:41.860
<v Maya>Why does that work, though?</v>

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:44.120
<v Maya>What's actually happening in the room?</v>

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:45.140
<v Marcus>Two things.</v>

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:47.380
<v Marcus>Memory and emotion.</v>

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:53.020
<v Marcus>Our brains are wired for narrative: a situation, a tension, a resolution.</v>

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.940
<v Marcus>And decisions are emotional first, rational second.</v>

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:00.500
<v Marcus>People decide with their gut and justify with data.</v>

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:02.700
<v Marcus>A story speaks to the gut.</v>

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:09.720
<v Marcus>That's why the same strategy, told as a story, gets funded, and told as a spreadsheet, gets filed.</v>

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.520
<v Maya>Where do you actually use this?</v>

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:13.020
<v Maya>Give me the moments.</v>

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.560
<v Marcus>The biggest one is the principal's narrative.</v>

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:19.880
<v Marcus>Every CEO has a vision, but most can't tell it as a story.</v>

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:21.920
<v Marcus>They tell it as a strategy.</v>

00:01:22.580 --> 00:01:28.740
<v Marcus>Your job is to help them shape it: where we were, what changed, where we're going, and why it matters.</v>

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.960
<v Marcus>That's the spine of every great company story.</v>

00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:39.940
<v Marcus>When the CEO can tell that in three minutes, in a hallway, without slides, the whole organization starts moving in the same direction.</v>

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:41.660
<v Maya>What about the board?</v>

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:43.780
<v Maya>That's numbers, not narratives.</v>

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.260
<v Marcus>And that's why most board presentations are forgettable.</v>

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:49.440
<v Marcus>The numbers matter, obviously.</v>

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:51.320
<v Marcus>But wrap them in the story.</v>

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:53.220
<v Marcus>We set out to do this.</v>

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:54.560
<v Marcus>Here's what happened.</v>

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:56.220
<v Marcus>Here's what we learned.</v>

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:58.100
<v Marcus>Here's where we're going.</v>

00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:02.640
<v Marcus>The directors will remember the arc long after they forget the figures.</v>

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.960
<v Marcus>And the figures land harder inside the arc, because now they mean something.</v>

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:10.820
<v Maya>What if you're just not a natural storyteller?</v>

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:13.700
<v Maya>Some people freeze at "tell me a story."</v>

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:15.920
<v Marcus>It's a craft, not a gift.</v>

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:20.600
<v Marcus>Learn the spine: situation, tension, resolution.</v>

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.840
<v Marcus>Practice on small stages before big ones.</v>

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:29.660
<v Marcus>Tell the story of a project in a team meeting before you tell the company story at a town hall.</v>

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.020
<v Marcus>And steal structure shamelessly.</v>

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.660
<v Marcus>Every good story you've ever heard uses the same bones.</v>

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:37.900
<v Marcus>The skill isn't inventing.</v>

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.880
<v Marcus>It's selecting: which details earn their place, which ones get cut.</v>

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:44.240
<v Maya>Hardest version.</v>

00:02:44.740 --> 00:02:46.540
<v Maya>You have to tell a story the numbers don't support.</v>

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:47.760
<v Maya>A bad quarter.</v>

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:48.460
<v Maya>Layoffs.</v>

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:50.080
<v Maya>How do you do that honestly?</v>

00:02:50.540 --> 00:02:52.000
<v Marcus>You don't invent a better story.</v>

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:54.520
<v Marcus>You tell the true one, with dignity.</v>

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:59.280
<v Marcus>Here's what happened, here's what we're doing, here's what it means for you.</v>

00:03:00.280 --> 00:03:01.400
<v Marcus>People can handle hard truth.</v>

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.080
<v Marcus>What they can't handle is feeling managed.</v>

00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:11.160
<v Marcus>Here's the uncomfortable truth: the fastest way to destroy trust is a cheerful story over a painful reality.</v>

00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:13.140
<v Marcus>Everybody in the room knows.</v>

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.180
<v Marcus>So give them honesty, give them the plan, give them respect.</v>

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:20.640
<v Marcus>That's the story that holds a team together in the bad times.</v>

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:22.220
<v Maya>And the vision story?</v>

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.160
<v Maya>The future that isn't true yet.</v>

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:26.760
<v Maya>Where's the line between aspiration and fiction?</v>

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:31.380
<v Marcus>Aspiration says: this is where we're going, and here's how.</v>

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:34.400
<v Marcus>Fiction says: we're already there.</v>

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:36.120
<v Marcus>The difference is the path.</v>

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:42.620
<v Marcus>A vision story earns belief by showing the next three steps, not by pretending we've arrived.</v>

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.360
<v Marcus>People will follow an honest climb.</v>

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:47.580
<v Marcus>They'll resent a painted summit.</v>

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:48.880
<v Maya>Let me recap.</v>

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:52.320
<v Maya>Data informs, stories persuade.</v>

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.740
<v Maya>Learn the spine: situation, tension, resolution.</v>

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:01.260
<v Maya>Help your principal tell the company story in three minutes, no slides.</v>

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:04.300
<v Maya>Wrap board numbers in narrative so they mean something.</v>

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.640
<v Maya>Tell hard truths with dignity, never cheerfully.</v>

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:12.540
<v Maya>And keep vision stories honest: show the climb, don't paint the summit.</v>

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:16.600
<v Marcus>The chief of staff who can tell the story becomes the voice of the mission.</v>

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:19.680
<v Marcus>And the voice of the mission is never out of work.</v>

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:25.800
<v Maya>If this episode helped, follow the show and tap five stars — it's how other chiefs of staff find us.</v>

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:31.400
<v Maya>I'm Maya, he's Marcus, and this is Chief of Staff Confidential.</v>

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:32.720
<v Maya>See you next week!</v>

